\DOC LOCAL_EPADD_SAFETY_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_EPADD_SAFETY_TAC : string -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Applies a named subroutine specification assumption to discharge one call in an
{eventually} goal, advancing the symbolic state from step {n} to step {n+1} and
replacing the pre-call state facts with post-call ones.

\DESCRIBE
{LOCAL_EPADD_SAFETY_TAC assump_name n} takes a string {assump_name}, the label
of the assumption in the goal that holds the universally quantified {ensures}
specification of the called subroutine, and an integer {n}, the index of the
current symbolic state, used to name the successor state introduced in the
resulting goal. It produces exactly one subgoal, whose conclusion keeps its
{eventually} form and property but takes the next state (index {n+1}) as its
state argument. Among the assumptions, the facts describing the state at index
{n} — program/code loading, register and memory reads, flag conditions, the
event-trace equation and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — are discharged
and replaced by the corresponding facts about the new state, with the
{MAYCHANGE} relation extended by the frame of the called subroutine and the
event trace extended by a fresh existentially-named event-list variable
{x1 ... xn} (a primed variant of the previous one); two new assumptions
characterising that variable are added, namely its definition in terms of the
subroutine's event function and a memory-access-inbounds property. The named
specification assumption is retained but moved to the end of the assumption
list; all other assumptions, including previously derived event-list facts and
nonoverlapping hypotheses, are untouched.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single pattern, on goals whose conclusion is an
{eventually} statement about a machine state and whose assumptions include the
named subroutine specification.

\ENDDOC
